IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 152 of 2013
CITATION: (2021) 4 SCC 1
YEAR: 2021

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: R.F. Nariman, Navin Sinha, Indira Banerjee

PARTIES:
Toofan Singh vs State of Tamil Nadu

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985, Indian Evidence Act, 1872

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 67 NDPS, Section 53 NDPS, Section 25 Evidence Act

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
confession to officers, inadmissible evidence, police officer status

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Three-judge bench held that officers designated under Section 53 NDPS are police officers; therefore, confessional statements recorded under Section 67 cannot be utilized as substantive evidence.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 67 NDPS, Section 53 NDPS, Section 25 Evidence Act.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

